<div class="page-prose"><h1>About PHP Hub</h1>
<p>PHP Hub is written by Raghav, a full-stack PHP, Laravel and Node developer based in Mohali, India, with 6 years of experience.</p>
<h2>What PHP Hub does</h2>
<p>The aim is simple: explain PHP and WordPress security issues in plain language, say clearly who is affected and what to do, and link to the original sources so you can check the facts yourself. Alongside the briefs there are lessons and articles for PHP and Laravel developers.</p>
<h2>How briefs are written</h2>
<ul><li>Every brief links to its primary sources (vendor advisories, php.net, CISA, researchers).</li><li>Severity and exploitation status are shown only when a source states them.</li><li>Unverified claims are labelled as such.</li></ul>
<h2>Editorial standards</h2>
<ul>
<li>Public pages are in English.</li>
<li>Briefs, lessons, and articles link the primary source when they state a fact about a vulnerability, a version, or a framework behavior.</li>
<li>Severity is shown when the source states it. A brief does not invent a score.</li>
<li>Lessons that were drafted with AI assistance say so on the page, next to the documents they were checked against.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Content review</h2>
<p>A brief is checked against the vendor advisory, php.net, CISA, or the researcher report named in that brief. Lessons that cite PHP, Laravel, Symfony, WordPress, or OWASP link those documents. If a claim is not verified, the post should say so. Corrections can be sent through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.</p>
<h2>Sources and references</h2>
<ul>
<li>php.net, including migration guides and PHP security advisories named in a post</li>
<li>Laravel and Symfony documentation and the advisories a post cites</li>
<li>WordPress and plugin-vendor advisories named in a brief</li>
<li>OWASP cheat sheets where a lesson cites them</li>
<li>NVD, the GitHub Advisory Database, and CISA KEV when a brief cites them</li>
</ul>
<h2>Disclaimer</h2>
<p>Educational content on PHP Hub is not a substitute for a professional, authorized security assessment of your application. Reading a lesson does not mean a site is secure. The <a href="/security">security dashboard</a> and the <a href="/tools">developer tools</a> are informational. Work on a system you do not own or administer is out of scope. See <a href="/authorized-work">authorized work</a> and <a href="/responsible-disclosure">responsible disclosure</a>.</p>
<h2>Work together</h2>
<p>Hands-on help with security audits, malware cleanup and PHP, Laravel or WordPress upgrades is available for sites you own or are authorized to manage. See <a href="/services">services</a> or <a href="/contact">get in touch</a>.</p></div></Base>
